wajo16_q10.solution:
Let \(u, v\) and \(w\) be the speeds of the first, second and third places, and let \(t_{1}\) be the time taken by first place. Then, since<br>$$
\begin{aligned}
\text { time } &amp; =\frac{\text { distance }}{\text { speed }} \\
\left(t_{1}=\right) \frac{5000}{u} &amp; =\frac{5000-200}{v}=\frac{5000-600}{w} \\
\therefore v &amp; =\frac{4800}{5000} u=\frac{24}{25} u \\
w &amp; =\frac{4400}{5000} u=\frac{22}{25} u
\end{aligned}
$$<br>Now let \(d\) be the distance between second and third at the time \(t_{2}\) when second place crosses the finish line. Then<br>$$
\begin{aligned}
\left(t_{2}=\right) \frac{5000}{v} &amp; =\frac{5000-d}{w} \\
5000 \cdot \frac{25}{24} \cdot \frac{1}{u} &amp; =(5000-d) \cdot \frac{25}{22} \cdot \frac{1}{u} \\
5000 \cdot \frac{22}{24} &amp; =5000-d \\
d &amp; =5000\left(1-\frac{22}{24}\right)=5000 \cdot \frac{2}{24}=\frac{1250}{3}=416 \frac{2}{3} \mathrm{~m},
\end{aligned}
$$<br>which rounds to 417 m.<br><b>Alternatively,</b> let \(t_{1}, u, v\) and \(w\) be as above. Then, as before<br>$$
\begin{aligned}
t_{1} &amp; =\frac{5000}{u}=\frac{4800}{v}=\frac{4400}{w} \\
\therefore v &amp; =\frac{4800}{t_{1}}, w=\frac{4400}{t_{1}}.
\end{aligned}
$$<br>To run 200 m, the second runner needs time<br>$$
\frac{200}{v}=200 \cdot \frac{t_{1}}{4800}=\frac{t_{1}}{24}.
$$<br>In that time, the third runner will run a distance of<br>$$
w \cdot \frac{t_{1}}{24}=\frac{4400}{t_{1}} \cdot \frac{t_{1}}{24}=\frac{4400}{24}=\frac{550}{3}=183 \frac{1}{3} \mathrm{~m}
$$<br>so that in the time the second runner runs the last 200 m, the third runner runs \(183 \frac{1}{3} \mathrm{~m}\). Before running those respective final distances the second and third runners were 400 m apart. So when the second runner crosses the finish line, the second and third runners are<br>$$
400+200-183 \frac{1}{3}=416 \frac{2}{3} \approx 417 \mathrm{~m}
$$<br>apart.
